1
00:00:01,368 --> 00:00:02,859
♪♪

2
00:02:13,333 --> 00:02:15,859
Hey, baby, where are you going?

3
00:02:16,003 --> 00:02:17,995
We've got a date, remember?

4
00:02:18,138 --> 00:02:19,606
I've got to get home.

5
00:02:19,740 --> 00:02:21,106
My son is sick.

6
00:02:21,241 --> 00:02:24,143
Oh, now, why would
anybody care about that brat?

7
00:02:24,277 --> 00:02:28,271
Unless, of course, you're
meeting somebody else.

8
00:02:28,415 --> 00:02:29,781
No.

9
00:02:29,916 --> 00:02:31,350
I think you're lying.

10
00:02:31,485 --> 00:02:32,485
No.

11
00:02:32,586 --> 00:02:35,681
You are lying!

12
00:02:38,125 --> 00:02:40,219
Leave her alone.

13
00:02:40,360 --> 00:02:41,555
Okay.

14
00:02:42,729 --> 00:02:44,960
Fine. No problem.

15
00:02:45,098 --> 00:02:48,262
Just having a little
difference of opinion, that's all.

16
00:02:48,402 --> 00:02:50,132
It's nothing for
you to worry about.

17
00:02:50,270 --> 00:02:52,102
Aah!

18
00:03:02,149 --> 00:03:04,209
He won't bother you again.

19
00:03:04,351 --> 00:03:05,614
Will you, punk?

20
00:03:06,853 --> 00:03:07,946
No.

21
00:03:08,088 --> 00:03:10,057
No, I promise.

22
00:03:10,190 --> 00:03:11,886
And I'll make sure
you keep that promise.

23
00:03:12,025 --> 00:03:13,152
Now get out of here.

24
00:03:14,795 --> 00:03:15,854
Okay.

25
00:03:15,996 --> 00:03:17,157
Fine.

26
00:03:17,297 --> 00:03:18,297
Thanks.

27
00:03:29,443 --> 00:03:30,968
Thank you.

28
00:03:32,913 --> 00:03:33,913
Jimmy.

29
00:03:34,981 --> 00:03:36,142
Jimmy?

30
00:03:36,283 --> 00:03:38,411
Yo, Jimmy.

31
00:03:38,552 --> 00:03:40,521
Jimmy.

32
00:03:40,654 --> 00:03:41,654
Hey, are you awake?

33
00:03:41,788 --> 00:03:45,816
Hey, sport, run some cocktail
napkins out to Vern for me, will you?

34
00:03:45,959 --> 00:03:46,999
My hands are kind of full.

35
00:03:47,027 --> 00:03:48,893
They're over here on the box.

36
00:03:49,029 --> 00:03:50,029
Okay.

37
00:03:57,838 --> 00:03:59,272
Atta boy.

38
00:03:59,406 --> 00:04:01,807
Thanks, champ.
You're a lifesaver.

39
00:04:01,942 --> 00:04:03,706
♪♪

40
00:04:15,155 --> 00:04:18,057
Put a lot of ice
in there, Lorraine.

41
00:04:18,191 --> 00:04:19,454
Here you go, Vern.

42
00:04:19,593 --> 00:04:21,687
It's about time. I got this one.

43
00:04:22,929 --> 00:04:24,261
Get them tapped.

44
00:04:24,397 --> 00:04:25,477
Here are the napkins, Vern.

45
00:04:25,565 --> 00:04:27,830
How about you come
around and open them?

46
00:04:30,704 --> 00:04:33,196
I need two drafts
and a Virgin Mary.

47
00:04:33,340 --> 00:04:35,571
Coming right up.

48
00:04:35,709 --> 00:04:36,836
Hi, Mom.

49
00:04:36,977 --> 00:04:39,173
What are you doing back there?

50
00:04:39,312 --> 00:04:40,712
He's helping me out.

51
00:04:40,847 --> 00:04:43,043
Ellie went home sick,
and I'm shorthanded.

52
00:04:43,183 --> 00:04:44,651
He's supposed to be sleeping.

53
00:04:44,785 --> 00:04:46,481
Tomorrow's a school day.

54
00:04:46,620 --> 00:04:49,852
Hey, you want him to
sleep, leave him home.

55
00:04:49,990 --> 00:04:52,653
This is a honkytonk,
not a dormitory.

56
00:04:52,793 --> 00:04:55,456
If you give me a raise
so I could pay a babysitter,

57
00:04:55,595 --> 00:04:57,063
I'd be glad to leave him home.

58
00:04:57,197 --> 00:04:58,597
But I like it here, Mom.

59
00:04:58,732 --> 00:05:00,792
Don't worry, champ. It
will be a cold day in hell

60
00:05:00,934 --> 00:05:03,335
before old Vern here
gives any of us a raise.

61
00:05:05,172 --> 00:05:07,232
Here you go, Mom.

62
00:05:07,374 --> 00:05:09,275
As soon as you're done,

63
00:05:09,409 --> 00:05:11,776
back to the storeroom
and get some sleep.

64
00:05:14,381 --> 00:05:16,021
Lorraine, darling,
can I get a beer?

65
00:05:16,116 --> 00:05:17,140
Thank you.

66
00:05:18,552 --> 00:05:20,783
Hey, doll, how are you doing?

67
00:05:20,921 --> 00:05:23,516
- I'm doing just fine.
- Yes, you are. You're doing fine.

68
00:05:23,657 --> 00:05:25,990
- What time do you get off?
- Not till closing.

69
00:05:26,126 --> 00:05:28,322
All right. I'll be waiting.

70
00:05:28,461 --> 00:05:29,793
What about her?

71
00:05:32,632 --> 00:05:35,329
Darling, I'm just teaching
her the finer points of pinball.

72
00:05:35,468 --> 00:05:36,468
That's all.

73
00:05:49,583 --> 00:05:51,023
Hang on, Conrad! Hang on, baby!

74
00:05:51,151 --> 00:05:53,780
Hey, Conrad, just
do it, Conrad! Do it!

75
00:05:58,592 --> 00:06:00,584
Ride 'em!

76
00:06:03,864 --> 00:06:05,594
All right!

77
00:06:09,102 --> 00:06:11,435
Hey, what do you say, Ann?

78
00:06:11,571 --> 00:06:13,563
You are really getting good.

79
00:06:13,707 --> 00:06:16,176
Well, you think I ought
to be a rodeo rider?

80
00:06:16,309 --> 00:06:18,642
I've been spending too
much time in this place.

81
00:06:18,778 --> 00:06:20,872
No, it seems like you're
hardly ever here anymore.

82
00:06:21,014 --> 00:06:23,574
Yeah, well, too busy, you know.

83
00:06:23,717 --> 00:06:25,515
We miss you.

84
00:06:26,853 --> 00:06:28,014
Thank you.

85
00:06:28,154 --> 00:06:30,146
- I've got to get.
- All right. See you.

86
00:06:30,290 --> 00:06:31,485
Hey, Hattie!

87
00:06:39,966 --> 00:06:41,832
Come on, buddy,
let's get you home.

88
00:06:41,968 --> 00:06:43,937
- Good night, you two.
- Good night, Walt.

89
00:06:45,705 --> 00:06:47,105
Come on.

90
00:06:47,240 --> 00:06:48,674
Good night, everybody.

91
00:06:48,808 --> 00:06:50,037
- Good night.
- Good night.

92
00:06:50,176 --> 00:06:52,407
Hey. Are you going my way?

93
00:06:52,545 --> 00:06:54,844
No. I don't think so.

94
00:06:54,981 --> 00:06:56,711
You know, you've
had an attitude all night.

95
00:06:56,850 --> 00:06:58,079
I'm surprised you noticed.

96
00:06:58,218 --> 00:06:59,413
What are you talking about?

97
00:06:59,552 --> 00:07:00,952
Us, Dwayne.

98
00:07:01,087 --> 00:07:03,454
You don't really care,
I don't really care.

99
00:07:03,590 --> 00:07:06,560
So why don't we just get on
with our lives and call it quits?

100
00:07:06,693 --> 00:07:09,754
That's it? It's
over, just like that?

101
00:07:09,896 --> 00:07:14,800
You can come by tomorrow
morning at 11:00 and pick up your stuff.

102
00:07:14,935 --> 00:07:17,029
I'll arrange to be out.

103
00:07:27,781 --> 00:07:28,976
Come on, honey.

104
00:07:30,150 --> 00:07:33,086
Hey, don't I get a
say in any of this?

105
00:07:33,219 --> 00:07:35,916
There is nothing between
us, Dwayne, and you know it.

106
00:07:36,056 --> 00:07:39,390
I think there is, and it ain't
over until I say it's over.

107
00:07:39,526 --> 00:07:41,722
Understand? Don't think
you're getting in that car.

108
00:07:41,861 --> 00:07:42,861
Let go of me.

109
00:07:42,929 --> 00:07:45,592
Get off me, you
little... Let go of me.

110
00:07:45,732 --> 00:07:46,893
Jimmy, stand back.

111
00:07:47,033 --> 00:07:48,626
Stay away from us,
Dwayne. I mean it.

112
00:07:48,768 --> 00:07:50,760
Do it, Mom. Get him.

113
00:07:50,904 --> 00:07:53,135
Baby, what are you
going to do with that?

114
00:07:58,244 --> 00:07:59,268
Dwayne.

115
00:08:01,514 --> 00:08:02,641
Come on, come on.

116
00:08:02,782 --> 00:08:04,216
Leave me alone.

117
00:08:04,351 --> 00:08:05,683
Hey!

118
00:08:07,921 --> 00:08:09,947
- Get off me.
- Leave him alone.

119
00:08:15,562 --> 00:08:16,562
Come on.

120
00:08:16,663 --> 00:08:19,258
- Ohh!
- Waste him, Walt! Waste him!

121
00:08:19,399 --> 00:08:20,458
Aah!

122
00:08:23,703 --> 00:08:25,171
Apologize to the lady.

123
00:08:28,408 --> 00:08:31,173
- I said apologize.
- All right. All right!

124
00:08:32,579 --> 00:08:34,309
I'm sorry.

125
00:08:34,447 --> 00:08:35,471
Sorry.

126
00:08:35,615 --> 00:08:38,881
You pick up your stuff tomorrow,
or it will be out on the street.

127
00:08:39,019 --> 00:08:43,457
And don't you ever lay a
hand on me or my son again.

128
00:09:35,575 --> 00:09:36,575
What the...?

129
00:09:37,644 --> 00:09:38,907
Aah!

130
00:09:52,392 --> 00:09:53,655
Aah!

131
00:09:55,395 --> 00:09:56,624
Geez.

132
00:10:00,900 --> 00:10:02,232
Mrs. Johnson?

133
00:10:04,471 --> 00:10:06,235
Dwayne?

134
00:10:07,540 --> 00:10:08,735
Mrs. Johnson?

135
00:10:12,579 --> 00:10:13,579
He...

136
00:10:17,951 --> 00:10:20,648
Why is it every time
I get a little time off

137
00:10:20,787 --> 00:10:24,315
to do a few things around the
house like change a few light bulbs,

138
00:10:24,457 --> 00:10:26,585
somebody I know
has a friend in trouble?

139
00:10:26,726 --> 00:10:29,753
Ben, you've got to defend
her. She's such a nice person.

140
00:10:29,896 --> 00:10:31,387
You said she's a waitress?

141
00:10:31,531 --> 00:10:33,363
It's the only job she could get.

142
00:10:33,500 --> 00:10:36,163
You know, the tips, and
she's got this little boy.

143
00:10:36,302 --> 00:10:38,669
Well, how about
a public defender?

144
00:10:38,805 --> 00:10:40,774
Ben, you've got to defend her.

145
00:10:40,907 --> 00:10:42,000
Look, you'll see.

146
00:10:42,142 --> 00:10:45,374
Ann Johnson is one of the
nicest, most hardworking,

147
00:10:45,512 --> 00:10:47,743
honest, and courageous
people I've ever met.

148
00:10:49,249 --> 00:10:51,480
Well, hell, Conrad,
instead of defending her,

149
00:10:51,618 --> 00:10:54,247
why don't I just nominate
her for president?

150
00:10:54,387 --> 00:10:57,380
Heh heh heh. You
character. Come on.

151
00:11:01,828 --> 00:11:03,091
Ann?

152
00:11:04,397 --> 00:11:06,229
Ann, I want you to
meet Ben Matlock,

153
00:11:06,366 --> 00:11:09,063
probably the best
lawyer there ever was...

154
00:11:09,202 --> 00:11:11,728
And a real nice man.

155
00:11:11,871 --> 00:11:14,466
Ben, Ann Johnson.

156
00:11:14,607 --> 00:11:16,838
- Hello.
- Hello.

157
00:11:20,046 --> 00:11:22,242
Well, why don't we all sit down?

158
00:11:22,382 --> 00:11:23,645
Okay.

159
00:11:37,597 --> 00:11:38,724
Uh...

160
00:11:39,732 --> 00:11:42,395
Well, what was...

161
00:11:42,535 --> 00:11:43,901
What was this fellow's name?

162
00:11:44,037 --> 00:11:46,506
Dwayne Meeks.
He was a real jerk.

163
00:11:49,909 --> 00:11:52,037
Were you living with him?

164
00:11:53,046 --> 00:11:54,537
Oh, not really.

165
00:11:54,681 --> 00:11:59,051
He'd stay over every once
in a while... Often enough.

166
00:11:59,185 --> 00:12:01,780
His stuff was
starting to pile up.

167
00:12:01,921 --> 00:12:04,366
Is that what he was doing there
that morning? Getting his stuff?

168
00:12:04,390 --> 00:12:05,449
Yeah.

169
00:12:05,592 --> 00:12:09,723
Let's see, um...

170
00:12:09,862 --> 00:12:14,664
you said that the police found
two little burn marks on his neck?

171
00:12:14,801 --> 00:12:16,770
Yeah, remember? They
think before he was murdered,

172
00:12:16,903 --> 00:12:18,667
he was stung with
one of those stun guns.

173
00:12:18,805 --> 00:12:20,000
Oh, yeah, yeah, yeah.

174
00:12:20,139 --> 00:12:23,075
A lot of people carry
those for defense.

175
00:12:23,209 --> 00:12:24,302
Do you carry one?

176
00:12:24,444 --> 00:12:27,209
I used to.

177
00:12:27,347 --> 00:12:31,808
Dwayne knocked it out of my
hand when I had that fight with him.

178
00:12:31,951 --> 00:12:35,046
Well, it's probably still
out there in the parking lot.

179
00:12:37,257 --> 00:12:41,456
Uh, how many people
saw you out there that night?

180
00:12:41,594 --> 00:12:46,589
Everybody who worked
till closing, I guess.

181
00:12:46,733 --> 00:12:49,134
They probably thought I
was getting what I deserved

182
00:12:49,269 --> 00:12:52,637
for having the worst taste in
men of any woman in history.

183
00:12:52,772 --> 00:12:54,365
Yeah, she has had bad luck.

184
00:12:54,507 --> 00:12:57,477
And like I said, that
Dwayne... He was a jerk.

185
00:12:57,610 --> 00:13:02,844
You show me a self-centered,
mean-spirited jackass,

186
00:13:02,982 --> 00:13:07,044
and chances are I've either
dated him or married him.

187
00:13:15,228 --> 00:13:18,323
Well, hey, that boy of yours,
he's a hell of an artist, huh?

188
00:13:18,464 --> 00:13:23,232
Oh, Jimmy's been drawing
ever since he could hold a pencil.

189
00:13:23,369 --> 00:13:27,238
Of course, a lot of that is because I've
had to leave him on his own so much.

190
00:13:27,373 --> 00:13:33,404
He got good at drawing because there
just plain wasn't anything else to do.

191
00:13:35,648 --> 00:13:37,708
Yeah, but he got good at it.

192
00:13:37,850 --> 00:13:39,876
Where is he now?

193
00:13:40,019 --> 00:13:41,146
At Walt Thomas'.

194
00:13:41,287 --> 00:13:42,287
He's a friend.

195
00:13:42,388 --> 00:13:44,482
He works at Vern's, too.

196
00:13:44,624 --> 00:13:48,959
You know, the worst thing about
being in here is being without Jimmy.

197
00:13:50,530 --> 00:13:53,227
My arms ache,
they feel so empty.

198
00:14:16,422 --> 00:14:19,654
How does it feel to have
murderer for a mom, Johnson?

199
00:14:19,792 --> 00:14:21,954
She's not a murderer.
She's innocent.

200
00:14:22,095 --> 00:14:23,688
She's in jail, isn't she?

201
00:14:23,830 --> 00:14:25,389
Yeah. She's going
to go to the chair,

202
00:14:25,531 --> 00:14:27,176
and you're going to
wind up in the orphanage

203
00:14:27,200 --> 00:14:28,610
with all of the other
murderers' kids.

204
00:14:28,634 --> 00:14:30,330
Shut up.

205
00:14:30,470 --> 00:14:32,063
Who's going to make us? You?

206
00:14:33,539 --> 00:14:35,167
I am, if I have to.

207
00:14:36,275 --> 00:14:37,538
It's Mr. Awesome!

208
00:14:37,677 --> 00:14:39,942
Let's get out of here!

209
00:14:46,319 --> 00:14:48,379
Everything's going
to be all right, Jimmy.

210
00:14:50,256 --> 00:14:51,315
Jimmy?

211
00:14:53,993 --> 00:14:55,825
- Can I come in?
- Sure.

212
00:14:56,863 --> 00:14:58,855
Lights out, champ. You've
got school tomorrow.

213
00:15:01,601 --> 00:15:03,331
Hey, what have you got there?

214
00:15:05,872 --> 00:15:07,568
Are you making a comic book?

215
00:15:12,145 --> 00:15:13,306
That's great.

216
00:15:16,315 --> 00:15:18,784
Mr. Awesome.

217
00:15:23,856 --> 00:15:25,484
That's me.

218
00:15:30,496 --> 00:15:31,496
Come on.

219
00:15:32,565 --> 00:15:34,158
- Let's go to bed.
- Right.

220
00:15:35,168 --> 00:15:36,329
Atta boy.

221
00:15:39,205 --> 00:15:40,229
Slap me five.

222
00:15:40,373 --> 00:15:41,397
You've got it.

223
00:15:45,645 --> 00:15:46,645
Zap.

224
00:15:49,715 --> 00:15:51,411
- Good night, champ.
- Good night.

225
00:16:09,469 --> 00:16:10,469
I don't believe it.

226
00:16:10,503 --> 00:16:11,664
Not Mrs. Johnson.

227
00:16:11,804 --> 00:16:13,170
She's a model tenant.

228
00:16:13,306 --> 00:16:17,266
She's always on time with the
rent, never plays her stereo too loud.

229
00:16:17,410 --> 00:16:18,901
Of course, she doesn't have one.

230
00:16:19,045 --> 00:16:21,708
Every penny she's got
goes to her boy's education.

231
00:16:21,848 --> 00:16:23,646
She's got a lot of
character, that lady.

232
00:16:23,783 --> 00:16:25,183
Yeah.

233
00:16:25,318 --> 00:16:27,398
Did you notice anybody
else coming or going that day?

234
00:16:27,520 --> 00:16:28,783
Just Dwayne.

235
00:16:28,921 --> 00:16:30,947
He drove up while I was
out mowing the front yard.

236
00:16:31,090 --> 00:16:33,992
I said hi. He just
walked right on past.

237
00:16:34,126 --> 00:16:35,719
The guy was a jerk.

238
00:16:35,862 --> 00:16:37,831
What she saw in
him is beyond me.

239
00:16:37,964 --> 00:16:41,662
I guess she figured the boy
needed a father figure or something.

240
00:16:41,801 --> 00:16:43,633
He needs a shrink,
if you ask me.

241
00:16:43,769 --> 00:16:45,931
He's always up there
on the roof drawing.

242
00:16:46,072 --> 00:16:47,563
- On the roof?
- Yeah.

243
00:16:47,707 --> 00:16:49,187
He's got a little
clubhouse up there.

244
00:16:49,308 --> 00:16:51,834
He made it out of little
scraps of wood I give him.

245
00:16:51,978 --> 00:16:54,874
I thought a taste of
carpentry might get

246
00:16:54,898 --> 00:16:58,111
him interested in, you
know, manly pursuits.

247
00:16:59,318 --> 00:17:00,809
- Hey, Ben.
- Hmm?

248
00:17:00,953 --> 00:17:02,319
Over here.

249
00:17:05,658 --> 00:17:07,354
This lock's broken.

250
00:17:07,493 --> 00:17:09,724
Yeah. That was
in the police report.

251
00:17:09,862 --> 00:17:12,388
Ann said it's broken
most of the time.

252
00:17:12,532 --> 00:17:13,659
Now wait a minute.

253
00:17:13,799 --> 00:17:16,268
Every time she tells me
it's broken, I fix it right away.

254
00:17:16,402 --> 00:17:17,922
I wanted to replace
it with a new one,

255
00:17:18,037 --> 00:17:19,681
but the owner's the
biggest tightwad in Atlanta.

256
00:17:19,705 --> 00:17:22,470
The guy makes a half a million
a year, and he still clips coupons.

257
00:17:22,608 --> 00:17:23,769
Can you believe that?

258
00:17:27,914 --> 00:17:31,146
Do you think the person that
killed Dwayne came in the back?

259
00:17:31,284 --> 00:17:34,379
According to him, Dwayne
had a key to the front door,

260
00:17:34,520 --> 00:17:37,251
and the back lock
was pretty flimsy.

261
00:17:37,390 --> 00:17:39,256
Hey, did Jimmy do these?

262
00:17:39,392 --> 00:17:40,655
Boy.

263
00:17:40,793 --> 00:17:43,126
They're not bad for
an 11-year-old, huh?

264
00:17:43,262 --> 00:17:47,029
No. He must be
some kind of genius.

265
00:17:47,166 --> 00:17:48,498
He's smart. Look at that.

266
00:17:48,634 --> 00:17:50,660
Those are the people
from Vern's Cowboy Palace.

267
00:17:50,803 --> 00:17:51,803
Huh.

268
00:17:51,938 --> 00:17:54,271
That's Lorraine Ortega...
she's a bartender.

269
00:17:54,407 --> 00:17:56,808
That's the owner, Vern Trudeau.

270
00:17:56,943 --> 00:17:59,936
That's Walt Thomas. That's
the guy Jimmy's staying with.

271
00:18:00,079 --> 00:18:01,274
That's Ann.

272
00:18:01,414 --> 00:18:03,246
And that's Dwayne Meeks.

273
00:18:03,382 --> 00:18:04,509
He looks mean.

274
00:18:04,650 --> 00:18:06,118
He was.

275
00:18:06,252 --> 00:18:08,483
I guess that's the
way Jimmy saw him.

276
00:18:09,522 --> 00:18:11,286
Yeah.

277
00:18:11,424 --> 00:18:13,416
Boy, this kid's into violence.

278
00:18:13,559 --> 00:18:16,961
Look at all this
fighting and zapping.

279
00:18:17,096 --> 00:18:18,155
Look at that.

280
00:18:18,297 --> 00:18:19,993
All little boys
draw stuff like that.

281
00:18:20,132 --> 00:18:20,861
I guess.

282
00:18:21,000 --> 00:18:23,265
I guess so.

283
00:18:29,609 --> 00:18:31,043
♪♪

284
00:18:39,352 --> 00:18:42,049
Hey, you see the little
filly there in tight jeans?

285
00:18:42,188 --> 00:18:44,214
She's got quite a hitch
in her git-along, huh?

286
00:19:08,781 --> 00:19:10,807
Hold on, hold on.

287
00:19:10,950 --> 00:19:12,475
Hey!

288
00:19:15,154 --> 00:19:17,555
Hang on, son, hang on, hang on.

289
00:19:20,593 --> 00:19:22,926
I'm all right. I'm all right.

290
00:19:23,062 --> 00:19:24,223
What's the matter with you?

291
00:19:24,363 --> 00:19:25,922
You distracted me.

292
00:19:26,065 --> 00:19:27,533
I'm actually very good at this.

293
00:19:27,667 --> 00:19:30,603
At what? Flying through the
air and landing on your head?

294
00:19:30,736 --> 00:19:33,501
No, at riding that thing. I
do it every time I come here.

295
00:19:33,639 --> 00:19:35,471
- Why?
- It's fun, Ben.

296
00:19:35,608 --> 00:19:36,906
You ought to try it some time.

297
00:19:37,043 --> 00:19:38,306
You're nuts, you know that?

298
00:19:38,444 --> 00:19:40,106
Hey, let's just
talk a little bit.

299
00:19:40,246 --> 00:19:42,238
You don't pay money
to do that, do you?

300
00:19:42,381 --> 00:19:43,381
Let's drop it.

301
00:19:43,516 --> 00:19:46,247
Did you talk to the owner?

302
00:19:46,385 --> 00:19:47,978
Yeah.

303
00:19:48,120 --> 00:19:51,420
Nobody found a stun
gun in the parking lot.

304
00:19:51,557 --> 00:19:53,025
Hey, Conrad.

305
00:19:53,159 --> 00:19:54,388
Hey, Lorraine.

306
00:19:54,527 --> 00:19:55,790
Would you like something?

307
00:19:55,928 --> 00:19:57,055
No, not now.

308
00:19:57,196 --> 00:19:59,062
Who is your good-looking friend?

309
00:19:59,198 --> 00:20:00,198
Here's your hat.

310
00:20:00,299 --> 00:20:04,134
Oh, I'm Ben
Matlock... Ann's lawyer.

311
00:20:05,371 --> 00:20:07,431
Are you going to be
able to get her off?

312
00:20:07,573 --> 00:20:09,007
I hope so.

313
00:20:09,141 --> 00:20:11,076
Dwayne had it
coming, that's for sure.

314
00:20:11,210 --> 00:20:14,476
I hear you used
to go out with him.

315
00:20:14,613 --> 00:20:17,208
Me and half the women here.

316
00:20:17,349 --> 00:20:19,750
Went through us one by one.

317
00:20:19,885 --> 00:20:22,855
The big stud servicing his herd.

318
00:20:24,623 --> 00:20:26,455
When did you break up?

319
00:20:27,760 --> 00:20:29,592
- About a month ago.
- Oh.

320
00:20:29,729 --> 00:20:31,698
Whose idea was it?

321
00:20:31,831 --> 00:20:33,163
Mine.

322
00:20:33,299 --> 00:20:35,359
How did he take it?

323
00:20:35,501 --> 00:20:38,699
Well, within a week, he
was shacking up with Ann.

324
00:20:40,506 --> 00:20:42,407
How did you take that?

325
00:20:42,541 --> 00:20:44,669
No skin off my hide.

326
00:20:44,810 --> 00:20:48,906
I wasn't his first, and Ann
wouldn't have been his last.

327
00:20:49,048 --> 00:20:54,009
That morning that Dwayne
died... What was it, about 11?

328
00:20:54,153 --> 00:20:58,488
- Yeah, about 11.
- 11. Uh, do you remember what...

329
00:20:58,624 --> 00:21:03,722
In bed, sleeping in and
rediscovering the joys of celibacy.

330
00:21:03,863 --> 00:21:05,832
Is Walt working tonight?

331
00:21:05,965 --> 00:21:07,331
He's supposed to be.

332
00:21:07,466 --> 00:21:09,678
Unless, of course, he's home
washing his socks and undies

333
00:21:09,702 --> 00:21:12,001
- or reading a story to Jimmy.
- Lorraine!

334
00:21:12,138 --> 00:21:15,006
Keep your pants on,
sugar, I'll be right there.

335
00:21:15,141 --> 00:21:16,473
Excuse me.

336
00:21:16,609 --> 00:21:17,872
Thanks, Lorraine.

337
00:21:22,648 --> 00:21:24,480
I told you this would
happen, didn't I?

338
00:21:24,617 --> 00:21:26,381
- Didn't I?
- Yeah. Yeah.

339
00:21:31,390 --> 00:21:33,450
All right, listen up.

340
00:21:33,592 --> 00:21:35,993
If I have to, next time
I'll break something.

341
00:21:36,128 --> 00:21:38,654
And if you talk,
you're dead meat.

342
00:21:38,798 --> 00:21:41,199
- You got that?
- Yeah. Yeah. Yeah.

343
00:22:01,320 --> 00:22:03,050
It's called a ten step.

344
00:22:13,899 --> 00:22:15,663
I bet I could do that.

345
00:22:19,705 --> 00:22:21,503
Maybe I'll do it with her.

346
00:22:21,640 --> 00:22:23,040
There's Walt. Come on.

347
00:22:26,512 --> 00:22:27,980
Hey, Walt.

348
00:22:28,113 --> 00:22:29,479
Hey, Conrad. How are you doing?

349
00:22:29,615 --> 00:22:30,615
All right.

350
00:22:30,683 --> 00:22:32,618
Walt Thomas, meet Ben Matlock.

351
00:22:32,751 --> 00:22:34,062
- Nice to meet you.
- How you doing?

352
00:22:34,086 --> 00:22:35,577
How's Ann holding up?

353
00:22:35,721 --> 00:22:37,383
Pretty well. Pretty well.

354
00:22:37,523 --> 00:22:39,082
Your having Jimmy here with you

355
00:22:39,225 --> 00:22:41,490
sure takes a load off her
shoulders, I'll tell you that.

356
00:22:41,627 --> 00:22:44,256
Oh, it's no problem.
He's a great kid.

357
00:22:45,364 --> 00:22:47,492
So what's going
to happen to her?

358
00:22:47,633 --> 00:22:50,364
Well, it all depends on
whether I can find out

359
00:22:50,502 --> 00:22:53,097
what really happened
to Dwayne Meeks.

360
00:22:53,239 --> 00:22:55,708
Did you know him very well?

361
00:22:55,841 --> 00:22:58,003
Yeah, he came in here
just about every night.

362
00:22:59,078 --> 00:23:01,206
A lot of people
said he was a jerk.

363
00:23:02,248 --> 00:23:04,945
- Yeah.
- He was a ladies' man, huh?

364
00:23:05,084 --> 00:23:08,885
Oh, the ladies liked him
all right, including Ann.

365
00:23:09,021 --> 00:23:10,887
I don't know why.

366
00:23:11,023 --> 00:23:13,549
Then again, I'm no expert
when it comes to women.

367
00:23:13,692 --> 00:23:15,923
Yeah, well, who is, yeah?

368
00:23:16,061 --> 00:23:19,429
You didn't approve
of him and Ann, huh?

369
00:23:19,565 --> 00:23:22,831
Well, let's just say I think she
could have done a whole lot better.

370
00:23:22,968 --> 00:23:25,836
Yo, Walt, get up here
quick. I need your help.

371
00:23:25,971 --> 00:23:27,496
One of the speakers went out.

372
00:23:27,640 --> 00:23:28,938
Yeah, just a minute.

373
00:23:29,074 --> 00:23:31,168
Is Jimmy here tonight?

374
00:23:31,310 --> 00:23:33,142
Yeah, he's in the storeroom.

375
00:23:33,279 --> 00:23:35,457
That's the first room on
your left, past the men's john.

376
00:23:35,481 --> 00:23:36,847
- Okay.
- He's doing his homework.

377
00:23:36,982 --> 00:23:37,982
- Yeah.
- Take care.

378
00:23:38,050 --> 00:23:39,050
All right.

379
00:23:39,118 --> 00:23:40,245
Uh, just wait.

380
00:23:42,988 --> 00:23:48,052
Hey, Jimmy, I'm Ben
Matlock, your mother's lawyer.

381
00:23:48,193 --> 00:23:49,718
Could I speak with
you for a minute?

382
00:23:49,862 --> 00:23:50,862
Yeah.

383
00:23:50,930 --> 00:23:53,365
Oh, don't put it away. I'd
like to see what you're doing.

384
00:23:53,499 --> 00:23:55,400
Do you mind?

385
00:23:55,534 --> 00:23:56,968
Are you going to make fun of me?

386
00:23:57,102 --> 00:23:58,434
No.

387
00:23:58,570 --> 00:24:02,564
The stuff I saw over at
your mom's house was great.

388
00:24:02,708 --> 00:24:05,872
I may ask you for your autograph,
but I'd never make fun of you.

389
00:24:06,011 --> 00:24:11,177
Oh, boy, you've got your
own comic book going here.

390
00:24:14,286 --> 00:24:19,782
Boy, that Mr. Awesome looks a
lot like Walt Thomas, doesn't he?

391
00:24:22,027 --> 00:24:23,757
Does he have super powers?

392
00:24:23,896 --> 00:24:26,730
Yeah. He can transmit
psychically generated energy

393
00:24:26,865 --> 00:24:28,527
through his central
nervous system

394
00:24:28,667 --> 00:24:32,160
and out his fingertips by
reversing his sensory receptors.

395
00:24:32,304 --> 00:24:34,398
Tssszz!

396
00:24:36,909 --> 00:24:38,571
He can zap people.

397
00:24:38,711 --> 00:24:40,339
Oh.

398
00:24:40,479 --> 00:24:43,472
Oh, boy, that would be nice to
have super powers, wouldn't it?

399
00:24:43,615 --> 00:24:46,449
I've always wished I
could fly like Superman.

400
00:24:46,585 --> 00:24:51,046
Just put my arms out and just
jump and fly anywhere I wanted to.

401
00:24:51,190 --> 00:24:55,355
Yeah. I'd like to have super strength
so I could beat up all the bad guys.

402
00:24:55,494 --> 00:24:56,928
- You would?
- Yeah.

403
00:24:57,062 --> 00:24:58,428
And I'd be invulnerable, too,

404
00:24:58,564 --> 00:25:01,398
so they could hit me all they
wanted and they could never hurt me.

405
00:25:01,533 --> 00:25:04,298
Did Dwayne Meeks ever hit you?

406
00:25:04,436 --> 00:25:07,634
He threw me down
in the parking lot once.

407
00:25:07,773 --> 00:25:10,208
You didn't like him
very much, did you?

408
00:25:11,777 --> 00:25:13,803
I hated him.

409
00:25:14,880 --> 00:25:16,473
Oh.

410
00:25:17,916 --> 00:25:19,851
- Ben?
- Yeah.

411
00:25:22,488 --> 00:25:25,014
I made some tuna
salad. Get some bread.

412
00:25:25,157 --> 00:25:26,250
No, thanks.

413
00:25:26,392 --> 00:25:29,260
There's hotdogs
and bologna and stuff.

414
00:25:29,395 --> 00:25:30,454
Not hungry.

415
00:25:30,596 --> 00:25:31,655
You're not?

416
00:25:34,033 --> 00:25:36,298
I had this sort of
feeling about Jimmy.

417
00:25:36,435 --> 00:25:37,630
What?

418
00:25:37,770 --> 00:25:39,810
I went to his school, and
I talked to his principal.

419
00:25:39,838 --> 00:25:41,101
Yeah?

420
00:25:41,240 --> 00:25:43,903
He wasn't in class
the day of the murder.

421
00:25:50,516 --> 00:25:51,916
Here it is.

422
00:25:52,051 --> 00:25:56,284
The Taj Mahal she ain't, but then
again, Paul Bunyan Jimmy ain't.

423
00:25:56,422 --> 00:25:57,788
You should have seen him.

424
00:25:57,923 --> 00:26:00,358
He could barely lift that saw,
let alone swing that hammer.

425
00:26:00,492 --> 00:26:03,553
Yeah, needs a
father, that's for sure.

426
00:26:03,695 --> 00:26:06,130
A father and about
five years of shop class.

427
00:26:06,265 --> 00:26:08,291
Do they teach shop
in school anymore?

428
00:26:08,434 --> 00:26:09,663
Probably not.

429
00:26:09,802 --> 00:26:12,362
I've got to get back to work.
If you need me, I'll be in 2B.

430
00:26:12,504 --> 00:26:14,564
The toilet just
won't stop flushing.

431
00:27:18,070 --> 00:27:19,868
Hello, Jimmy.

432
00:27:20,005 --> 00:27:21,473
Hi, Mr. Matlock.

433
00:27:22,508 --> 00:27:24,306
- I'll meet you guys downstairs.
- Okay.

434
00:27:25,511 --> 00:27:29,107
So, uh, how was school?

435
00:27:29,248 --> 00:27:30,773
- Fine.
- Mm-hmm.

436
00:27:30,916 --> 00:27:33,545
I talked to your
principal the other day.

437
00:27:33,685 --> 00:27:38,521
He said you don't
always make it to school.

438
00:27:38,657 --> 00:27:40,285
Is that right?

439
00:27:40,425 --> 00:27:41,791
Yeah.

440
00:27:41,927 --> 00:27:46,262
He said you weren't there
the morning Dwayne died.

441
00:27:47,566 --> 00:27:49,330
Where were you? Up here?

442
00:27:52,337 --> 00:27:54,238
I found the stun gun.

443
00:28:03,582 --> 00:28:05,448
What did you do with it?

444
00:28:05,584 --> 00:28:07,416
I took it to the police.

445
00:28:07,553 --> 00:28:09,078
Why did you do that?

446
00:28:09,221 --> 00:28:11,486
What if they find
fingerprints or something?

447
00:28:11,623 --> 00:28:14,957
Whose fingerprints are you afraid
they'll find on it, Jimmy? Yours?

448
00:28:15,093 --> 00:28:16,755
No.

449
00:28:16,895 --> 00:28:18,591
Whose, then?

450
00:28:18,730 --> 00:28:23,031
I know you were up here that
day, Jimmy. Tell me what happened.

451
00:28:24,903 --> 00:28:28,135
I was afraid that Dwayne
might hurt Mom again,

452
00:28:28,273 --> 00:28:33,405
so I hid up here instead of going
to school and kind of kept watch.

453
00:28:33,545 --> 00:28:36,379
Mom was still gone
by the time he got here,

454
00:28:36,515 --> 00:28:39,349
so I just came up and
messed around for a while.

455
00:28:39,484 --> 00:28:41,953
But then I heard somebody
open the back door to our place,

456
00:28:42,087 --> 00:28:44,647
so I tried to see who
it was, but I couldn't.

457
00:28:44,790 --> 00:28:47,259
I went downstairs to go inside,

458
00:28:47,392 --> 00:28:49,588
and while I was
unlocking the front door,

459
00:28:49,728 --> 00:28:51,856
I could hear somebody
run out the back door.

460
00:28:53,432 --> 00:28:57,665
So by the time I got inside, the
only person I saw was Dwayne,

461
00:28:57,803 --> 00:28:59,897
and I could tell right
away that he was dead.

462
00:29:01,540 --> 00:29:03,008
Where was the stun gun?

463
00:29:03,141 --> 00:29:05,110
It was on the floor next to him.

464
00:29:05,244 --> 00:29:07,076
Why did you pick it up?

465
00:29:07,212 --> 00:29:08,544
Because...

466
00:29:10,682 --> 00:29:12,617
Did you think your
mom had killed him?

467
00:29:12,751 --> 00:29:14,310
No.

468
00:29:14,453 --> 00:29:16,388
When I looked over,
her car was gone,

469
00:29:16,521 --> 00:29:19,787
and she would have come
in the front door, not the back.

470
00:29:21,093 --> 00:29:22,823
Who are you protecting, Jimmy?

471
00:29:24,363 --> 00:29:25,592
Jimmy?

472
00:29:25,731 --> 00:29:27,256
I don't want to talk anymore.

473
00:29:27,399 --> 00:29:31,996
Well, sooner or later,

474
00:29:32,137 --> 00:29:35,972
you're going to have to tell
the police what you just told me.

475
00:30:27,959 --> 00:30:30,155
You let that be a lesson to you.

476
00:30:31,163 --> 00:30:32,163
Lorraine.

477
00:30:32,230 --> 00:30:33,323
Conrad.

478
00:30:33,465 --> 00:30:35,024
I never knew you
could ride like that.

479
00:30:35,167 --> 00:30:36,465
Well, hell, I didn't, either.

480
00:30:36,601 --> 00:30:38,379
It looked like you were
born on one of them things.

481
00:30:38,403 --> 00:30:40,668
It just goes to show, you
never know what you can do

482
00:30:40,806 --> 00:30:43,674
till somebody bets you
300 bucks you can't.

483
00:30:43,809 --> 00:30:46,836
Whoa, sugar bait. I
know you from someplace.

484
00:30:46,978 --> 00:30:48,537
Your dreams, more than likely.

485
00:30:48,680 --> 00:30:50,342
Waco, that's it.

486
00:30:50,482 --> 00:30:52,110
You used to live in Waco, Texas.

487
00:30:52,250 --> 00:30:53,250
Waco?

488
00:30:53,385 --> 00:30:55,581
In your bad dreams, doll.

489
00:30:58,323 --> 00:30:59,814
Hey, is Walt around?

490
00:30:59,958 --> 00:31:01,551
Nope. He just got off.

491
00:31:01,693 --> 00:31:03,355
- Tell me something?
- Sure.

492
00:31:03,495 --> 00:31:05,259
How did he get
along with Dwayne?

493
00:31:05,397 --> 00:31:06,660
Oh.

494
00:31:06,798 --> 00:31:10,360
After Dwayne started sleeping up
there with Ann, what do you think?

495
00:31:10,502 --> 00:31:15,202
See, Walt doesn't know
it, but he's got a problem.

496
00:31:15,340 --> 00:31:16,865
What's that?

497
00:31:17,008 --> 00:31:18,374
He's in love with Ann.

498
00:31:20,112 --> 00:31:22,081
It's obvious to
everybody but him.

499
00:31:22,214 --> 00:31:23,739
Dwayne knew it.

500
00:31:23,882 --> 00:31:26,716
That night, after Walt beat
him up in the parking lot,

501
00:31:26,852 --> 00:31:30,448
Dwayne went on and on to everybody
about how he was going to get her back,

502
00:31:30,589 --> 00:31:33,024
how nobody was ever
going to have her but him.

503
00:31:33,158 --> 00:31:35,389
You know, just
rubbing Walt's nose in it.

504
00:31:35,527 --> 00:31:36,654
Ann never told me that.

505
00:31:36,795 --> 00:31:39,424
It happened after she drove off.

506
00:31:39,564 --> 00:31:42,693
I thought old Walt was going to
pop a blood vessel, he got so mad.

507
00:31:44,569 --> 00:31:46,697
What can I get you?

508
00:31:46,838 --> 00:31:48,170
Nothing. Nothing.

509
00:31:48,306 --> 00:31:50,468
- Good ride.
- Thank you.

510
00:31:53,879 --> 00:31:55,370
Now that I've got a kid around,

511
00:31:55,514 --> 00:31:57,813
I always seem to be
about a half a length behind.

512
00:31:57,949 --> 00:31:58,949
Yeah.

513
00:31:59,084 --> 00:32:00,643
I don't know how
single parents do it.

514
00:32:00,786 --> 00:32:04,416
It's given me a whole new
respect for Annie, that's for sure.

515
00:32:04,556 --> 00:32:06,787
You're pretty fond of her.

516
00:32:06,925 --> 00:32:08,325
Oh, yeah.

517
00:32:08,460 --> 00:32:11,828
Were you a little jealous
when she took up with Dwayne?

518
00:32:11,963 --> 00:32:15,957
I got no right to be jealous. She
can take up with whoever she wants.

519
00:32:16,101 --> 00:32:19,367
Do you remember where you
were the morning he was killed?

520
00:32:23,642 --> 00:32:24,974
You think I killed him?

521
00:32:25,110 --> 00:32:27,841
Well, you had that fight
with him the night before.

522
00:32:27,979 --> 00:32:30,858
Yeah. And if I wanted to kill him,
I'd have done it right then and there.

523
00:32:30,882 --> 00:32:32,874
I certainly wouldn't
try to frame Annie for it.

524
00:32:33,018 --> 00:32:35,954
Oh, maybe you didn't
mean to frame Ann.

525
00:32:36,087 --> 00:32:37,953
Maybe you didn't
mean to kill Dwayne.

526
00:32:38,089 --> 00:32:39,849
Maybe you just wanted
to have it out with him

527
00:32:39,925 --> 00:32:41,518
and things went
a little too far.

528
00:32:41,660 --> 00:32:44,630
Maybe you had the stun gun and
were going to give it back to Ann

529
00:32:44,763 --> 00:32:47,062
and used it on Dwayne
without even thinking.

530
00:32:47,199 --> 00:32:50,033
That's a bunch of bull.

531
00:32:50,168 --> 00:32:54,333
Well, if you'll tell me where you were
that morning, maybe I'll quit slinging it.

532
00:32:56,308 --> 00:32:58,607
I was with someone.

533
00:32:58,743 --> 00:33:00,439
A woman?

534
00:33:02,247 --> 00:33:03,271
Yeah.

535
00:33:04,349 --> 00:33:06,784
A married woman.

536
00:33:06,918 --> 00:33:09,217
I'm going to have
to have her name.

537
00:33:11,556 --> 00:33:14,355
Tina. Tina Sadowski.

538
00:33:14,493 --> 00:33:16,018
Tina...

539
00:33:31,443 --> 00:33:33,344
Mrs. Sadowski?

540
00:33:33,478 --> 00:33:36,539
My name's Conrad McMasters.
Thanks a lot for coming.

541
00:33:36,681 --> 00:33:39,082
Could we make this quick, you
know, on account of my husband?

542
00:33:39,217 --> 00:33:41,482
- He's been getting real suspicious lately.
- Sure. Sure.

543
00:33:41,620 --> 00:33:45,785
Let's see, you and Walt have
been seeing each other for how long?

544
00:33:45,924 --> 00:33:47,085
About six months...

545
00:33:47,225 --> 00:33:49,160
I mean weeks... about six weeks.

546
00:33:49,294 --> 00:33:50,853
Six weeks.

547
00:33:50,996 --> 00:33:52,464
I already told you that.

548
00:33:52,597 --> 00:33:54,566
How did you two meet?

549
00:33:54,699 --> 00:33:57,168
- How did we meet?
- Uh-huh.

550
00:33:57,302 --> 00:34:00,295
She came in here one
night with a bunch of ladies,

551
00:34:00,438 --> 00:34:02,998
and I guess we
just started talking.

552
00:34:03,141 --> 00:34:04,268
At the bar.

553
00:34:04,409 --> 00:34:06,969
We just started
talking one night.

554
00:34:07,112 --> 00:34:09,741
Where was your husband?

555
00:34:09,881 --> 00:34:11,349
He was working.

556
00:34:11,483 --> 00:34:12,712
What does he do?

557
00:34:12,851 --> 00:34:14,649
He sells real estate.

558
00:34:14,786 --> 00:34:16,277
He sells real estate.

559
00:34:16,421 --> 00:34:18,185
Oh. What's his name again?

560
00:34:19,224 --> 00:34:20,522
- Joe.
- Joe.

561
00:34:20,659 --> 00:34:22,059
Joe.

562
00:34:22,193 --> 00:34:24,890
And where do you and
Walt usually rendezvous?

563
00:34:25,030 --> 00:34:26,498
At his place.

564
00:34:26,631 --> 00:34:29,100
At a motel. Look, you're
kind of holding me up here.

565
00:34:29,234 --> 00:34:31,100
Look, I really do
have to get going.

566
00:34:31,236 --> 00:34:33,364
- Well, thank you for your time.
- Don't mention it.

567
00:34:33,505 --> 00:34:34,905
Thanks for coming.

568
00:34:46,718 --> 00:34:48,243
How are you doing, Al?

569
00:34:48,386 --> 00:34:50,184
Hey, it can't be time already.

570
00:34:50,322 --> 00:34:53,383
I'm afraid so. Mr. Newstead
wants his money.

571
00:34:53,525 --> 00:34:55,323
I don't have it.

572
00:34:55,460 --> 00:34:58,328
Al, you don't pay up this time,
then I've got to break something.

573
00:34:58,463 --> 00:35:00,908
Look, just give me one more day,
okay? I'll have it tomorrow. I swear.

574
00:35:00,932 --> 00:35:02,867
You know the rules, Al.

575
00:35:04,035 --> 00:35:06,129
Here... 180 bucks.

576
00:35:06,271 --> 00:35:08,035
Everything I've got. Take it.

577
00:35:08,173 --> 00:35:09,334
You owe 250.

578
00:35:09,474 --> 00:35:11,786
What do you want me to do? Do
you want me to pull it out of a hat?

579
00:35:11,810 --> 00:35:13,176
I don't have it.

580
00:35:14,746 --> 00:35:16,044
I'm sorry, Al.

581
00:35:16,181 --> 00:35:17,825
Wait, wait. The left
one, okay? The left one,

582
00:35:17,849 --> 00:35:20,478
so I can still play ball
with my kids, okay?

583
00:35:20,619 --> 00:35:22,053
Please?

584
00:35:29,227 --> 00:35:31,423
All right. I'll tell you what.

585
00:35:31,563 --> 00:35:33,862
Give me what you've got,
and I'll throw in the extra 70.

586
00:35:33,999 --> 00:35:36,730
- Thanks.
- I want it back in one week.

587
00:35:36,868 --> 00:35:39,030
You screw up, and I'll
break both your arms.

588
00:35:39,170 --> 00:35:40,934
You've got it. I swear.

589
00:35:47,612 --> 00:35:49,274
What are you doing here?

590
00:35:49,414 --> 00:35:52,248
Eavesdropping.

591
00:35:52,384 --> 00:35:53,818
How much did you hear?

592
00:35:53,952 --> 00:35:55,352
Everything.

593
00:35:57,422 --> 00:36:02,292
When did a nice fellow like you
start breaking legs for loan sharks?

594
00:36:05,864 --> 00:36:08,424
I bet on a few too
many horses last year.

595
00:36:08,566 --> 00:36:10,432
I owe Eddie
Newstead five figures.

596
00:36:10,568 --> 00:36:11,831
He's letting me work it off.

597
00:36:11,970 --> 00:36:14,462
It's not something
I'm real proud of.

598
00:36:14,606 --> 00:36:18,509
I've been following you ever
since it became apparent to me

599
00:36:18,643 --> 00:36:21,340
that you and Tina were
not having an affair.

600
00:36:21,479 --> 00:36:23,914
Tina's a friend.

601
00:36:24,049 --> 00:36:25,381
She's not married.

602
00:36:25,517 --> 00:36:27,509
She's just trying
to help me out.

603
00:36:27,652 --> 00:36:32,022
So what were you really doing
the morning Dwayne was killed?

604
00:36:33,591 --> 00:36:35,457
Breaking a man's collarbone.

605
00:36:35,593 --> 00:36:39,086
He was $85 short on a payment.

606
00:36:41,366 --> 00:36:43,232
Remember his name?

607
00:36:43,368 --> 00:36:46,702
Yeah. Tom Blackburn.

608
00:36:46,838 --> 00:36:51,799
I guess what I do on my spare time makes
adultery look downright noble, doesn't it?

609
00:36:51,943 --> 00:36:53,707
Don't tell Jimmy
about this, huh?

610
00:36:53,845 --> 00:36:55,609
He looks up to me.

611
00:36:55,747 --> 00:36:59,343
Hell, I'm the hero in that comic
book he's always working on.

612
00:37:01,086 --> 00:37:03,578
There's no reason he
needs to know, right?

613
00:37:07,025 --> 00:37:08,857
No, I guess not.

614
00:37:08,993 --> 00:37:11,394
Thank you.

615
00:37:11,529 --> 00:37:13,395
I mean it, thanks.

616
00:37:27,412 --> 00:37:30,041
Jimmy, I want you
to think real hard.

617
00:37:30,181 --> 00:37:33,743
When you looked to see who was at
the back door, did you see anything at all?

618
00:37:33,885 --> 00:37:37,981
I mean, like their clothes
or their hair or anything?

619
00:37:38,123 --> 00:37:41,560
By the time I got to the
edge, they were already inside.

620
00:37:41,693 --> 00:37:43,719
What about when you
unlocked the front door?

621
00:37:43,862 --> 00:37:45,728
You said you heard footsteps.

622
00:37:45,864 --> 00:37:47,332
What did their shoes sound like?

623
00:37:47,465 --> 00:37:50,697
Were they like boots...
loud... Or sneakers... soft?

624
00:37:52,337 --> 00:37:53,805
I don't remember.

625
00:37:53,938 --> 00:37:58,000
Well, I guess you weren't
meant to be a private detective,

626
00:37:58,143 --> 00:38:01,272
but I'll tell you one
thing... You sure can draw.

627
00:38:01,412 --> 00:38:04,871
You've got an eye for detail.

628
00:38:05,016 --> 00:38:07,110
I just draw what's there.

629
00:38:07,252 --> 00:38:08,345
Yeah.

630
00:38:15,126 --> 00:38:17,425
Do you know when you drew these?

631
00:38:17,562 --> 00:38:19,463
I always put the
date on the back.

632
00:38:25,236 --> 00:38:26,397
Hello.

633
00:38:28,072 --> 00:38:29,072
Yeah.

634
00:38:29,140 --> 00:38:31,371
Mr. Matlock, it's for you.

635
00:38:31,509 --> 00:38:32,568
Huh.

636
00:38:35,079 --> 00:38:37,548
- It's Conrad. He sounds excited.
- Yeah?

637
00:38:37,682 --> 00:38:39,241
Yeah, Conrad.

638
00:38:42,620 --> 00:38:44,111
Yeah, that's it.

639
00:38:44,255 --> 00:38:46,190
That's it.

640
00:38:46,324 --> 00:38:47,917
Thanks. Bye.

641
00:38:54,165 --> 00:38:59,502
Ms. Ortega, did you know
the deceased, Dwayne Meeks?

642
00:38:59,637 --> 00:39:01,629
Everybody at Vern's knew him.

643
00:39:01,773 --> 00:39:03,173
You went out with him.

644
00:39:03,308 --> 00:39:04,367
For a while, yeah.

645
00:39:04,509 --> 00:39:05,636
We broke up.

646
00:39:05,777 --> 00:39:06,836
When?

647
00:39:06,978 --> 00:39:09,607
About a month before he died.

648
00:39:09,747 --> 00:39:12,046
Did you live with him?

649
00:39:12,183 --> 00:39:13,183
No.

650
00:39:13,251 --> 00:39:14,685
No?

651
00:39:14,819 --> 00:39:19,154
What about all these
checks you wrote to him?

652
00:39:19,290 --> 00:39:21,657
See, here's one for 700,

653
00:39:21,793 --> 00:39:24,388
and here's one for 300,

654
00:39:24,529 --> 00:39:27,727
and here's one for 500.

655
00:39:27,866 --> 00:39:30,267
And there's more.

656
00:39:30,401 --> 00:39:33,337
Are you sure they weren't
house payments or something?

657
00:39:33,471 --> 00:39:34,471
No.

658
00:39:34,606 --> 00:39:36,472
Well, what were they for?

659
00:39:36,608 --> 00:39:39,772
He... They were loans.

660
00:39:39,911 --> 00:39:41,379
Loans?

661
00:39:41,512 --> 00:39:44,277
But he never paid you back.

662
00:39:44,415 --> 00:39:51,515
See, I subpoenaed his bank statements, too,
and all that money went from you to him.

663
00:39:51,656 --> 00:39:55,058
See that? Never from him to you.

664
00:39:55,193 --> 00:39:56,320
Not once.

665
00:39:56,461 --> 00:39:57,929
How come?

666
00:39:58,062 --> 00:40:01,191
How come, Ms. Ortega?

667
00:40:03,268 --> 00:40:04,861
They weren't loans, were they?

668
00:40:05,003 --> 00:40:07,939
They were blackmail
payments, weren't they?

669
00:40:08,072 --> 00:40:10,098
Why would Dwayne
be blackmailing me?

670
00:40:10,241 --> 00:40:13,678
Because he found
out who you really are.

671
00:40:13,811 --> 00:40:16,838
Not Lorraine Ortega
from Memphis, Tennessee,

672
00:40:16,981 --> 00:40:20,281
but Cissy Lockwood
from Amarillo, Texas...

673
00:40:21,452 --> 00:40:25,856
The girl the West
Texas Rodeo Association

674
00:40:25,990 --> 00:40:31,930
voted Cowgirl of the Year 1985.

675
00:40:32,063 --> 00:40:33,326
See that?

676
00:40:33,464 --> 00:40:36,457
Mr. Conrad
McMasters, over there,

677
00:40:36,601 --> 00:40:39,662
got curious when he saw
you riding that mechanical bull,

678
00:40:39,804 --> 00:40:43,002
and he overheard that cowboy
say he knew you from Waco.

679
00:40:43,141 --> 00:40:45,167
So he did some investigating.

680
00:40:45,310 --> 00:40:47,370
And sure enough, he found out

681
00:40:47,512 --> 00:40:53,611
that you indeed were on the
rodeo circuit back in Texas.

682
00:40:53,751 --> 00:40:55,743
Barrel racing, right?

683
00:40:55,887 --> 00:40:57,685
And what does that have to do...

684
00:40:57,822 --> 00:41:03,227
He also found out something else
that Dwayne probably found out...

685
00:41:03,361 --> 00:41:08,163
That there's an outstanding
warrant for your arrest back in Texas.

686
00:41:11,602 --> 00:41:14,800
You fell in with a pretty bad
dude about four years ago,

687
00:41:14,939 --> 00:41:18,637
and the two of you robbed a gas
station, nearly killed the attendant.

688
00:41:18,776 --> 00:41:21,473
And you drove
the car to get away.

689
00:41:21,612 --> 00:41:22,875
I did not.

690
00:41:23,014 --> 00:41:25,259
That's what the fellow told the
police when they caught up to him.

691
00:41:25,283 --> 00:41:27,445
- He's lying.
- Nevertheless, you're a fugitive.

692
00:41:27,585 --> 00:41:30,749
I imagine that's why you changed your
name when you moved here to Atlanta

693
00:41:30,888 --> 00:41:34,484
and why you gave Dwayne all that
money so he wouldn't tell the authorities.

694
00:41:34,625 --> 00:41:39,962
But when you realized the payments
were never going to stop, you killed him.

695
00:41:41,032 --> 00:41:42,466
None of that is true.

696
00:41:42,600 --> 00:41:46,128
You knew he would be at Ann's
house by himself that morning.

697
00:41:46,270 --> 00:41:47,932
You already had the stun gun...

698
00:41:48,072 --> 00:41:51,770
You picked it up probably off the pavement
where he knocked it out of Ann's hand.

699
00:41:51,909 --> 00:41:53,844
You slipped in
through her back door.

700
00:41:53,978 --> 00:41:57,210
You knew she had a bad lock because
she complained about it all the time.

701
00:41:57,348 --> 00:41:59,874
You used the stun gun
to incapacitate Dwayne,

702
00:42:00,018 --> 00:42:01,987
then you killed him
with a blow to the head.

703
00:42:02,120 --> 00:42:04,749
- Your Honor...
- Objection.

704
00:42:04,889 --> 00:42:07,051
This is pure,
unadulterated speculation.

705
00:42:07,191 --> 00:42:09,660
This is not speculation,
Your Honor.

706
00:42:09,794 --> 00:42:12,161
This is not speculation.

707
00:42:15,566 --> 00:42:20,027
Look at all the
facts we have so far.

708
00:42:20,171 --> 00:42:24,973
She changed her name to
start a new life here in Atlanta.

709
00:42:25,109 --> 00:42:30,013
She was barrel racing champion,
Cowgirl of the Year... kept that secret.

710
00:42:30,148 --> 00:42:35,553
She helped a guy rob a gas station,
almost murdering the attendant.

711
00:42:35,686 --> 00:42:38,212
Drove the car to get away.

712
00:42:38,356 --> 00:42:40,257
She's a fugitive.

713
00:42:40,391 --> 00:42:42,986
All those are facts.

714
00:42:43,127 --> 00:42:45,528
Don't you imagine there's more?

715
00:42:45,663 --> 00:42:47,791
We know all you've
just said, Mr. Matlock.

716
00:42:47,932 --> 00:42:51,460
Now get on with it, and it
better not be speculation.

717
00:42:51,602 --> 00:42:52,602
All right.

718
00:42:54,439 --> 00:42:55,532
How about this?

719
00:42:55,673 --> 00:42:57,141
What about it?

720
00:42:57,275 --> 00:43:01,144
This is pure,
unadulterated proof.

721
00:43:01,279 --> 00:43:02,713
It better be.

722
00:43:04,816 --> 00:43:10,346
Would you please tell the
judge and the court what this is?

723
00:43:10,488 --> 00:43:12,184
It's a belt buckle.

724
00:43:12,323 --> 00:43:14,417
Not just any belt buckle.

725
00:43:14,559 --> 00:43:17,586
This is the belt
buckle they gave you

726
00:43:17,728 --> 00:43:24,259
when you were voted American
Barrel Racing Champion in 1987.

727
00:43:24,402 --> 00:43:25,426
Right?

728
00:43:25,570 --> 00:43:27,004
- Yes.
- Yeah.

729
00:43:27,138 --> 00:43:31,439
The police found it in your apartment
this morning... with a warrant, of course.

730
00:43:31,576 --> 00:43:34,910
And the reason I subpoenaed it

731
00:43:35,046 --> 00:43:40,144
was because of these drawings
done by Jimmy Johnson.

732
00:43:40,284 --> 00:43:41,284
See?

733
00:43:41,419 --> 00:43:45,982
Now, here's a picture
of you wearing the belt.

734
00:43:46,124 --> 00:43:47,124
See there?

735
00:43:47,191 --> 00:43:52,596
This is in July before you
started going with Dwayne.

736
00:43:52,730 --> 00:43:53,959
See there?

737
00:43:55,399 --> 00:44:03,399
Now, these two are pictures of
Dwayne wearing the belt buckle

738
00:44:03,608 --> 00:44:05,941
just before you
broke up with him.

739
00:44:06,077 --> 00:44:07,077
See there?

740
00:44:07,145 --> 00:44:12,243
Yet the police found it in
your possession this morning.

741
00:44:12,383 --> 00:44:13,383
How could that be?

742
00:44:13,518 --> 00:44:15,487
Your Honor, these
are just drawings.

743
00:44:15,620 --> 00:44:19,182
They're drawings by an
11-year-old kid, for crying out loud.

744
00:44:19,323 --> 00:44:20,916
I've always had the buckle.

745
00:44:21,058 --> 00:44:22,856
You sure this wasn't
a blackmail payment?

746
00:44:22,994 --> 00:44:24,656
- No.
- A gift, maybe?

747
00:44:24,795 --> 00:44:27,765
I won that belt buckle because I
used to be the best at something.

748
00:44:27,899 --> 00:44:30,459
I'd have never given it to
somebody like Dwayne ever.

749
00:44:30,601 --> 00:44:33,264
Then why was he wearing
it when he was killed?

750
00:44:34,338 --> 00:44:35,772
He wasn't wearing it.

751
00:44:35,907 --> 00:44:37,170
Oh, but he was.

752
00:44:38,943 --> 00:44:45,372
Old Dwayne, as you know,
had quite a way with the girls.

753
00:44:45,516 --> 00:44:48,145
And when Ann wouldn't
sleep with him that night,

754
00:44:48,286 --> 00:44:51,415
he just went over to
Sally Redmond's house.

755
00:44:51,556 --> 00:44:52,819
Slept with her.

756
00:44:54,392 --> 00:44:56,327
She told me. Yeah.

757
00:44:56,460 --> 00:44:58,554
She said that he was...

758
00:44:59,597 --> 00:45:04,262
But anyway, he left there
the morning he was killed.

759
00:45:05,903 --> 00:45:10,739
Sally's sitting right over there,
ready, willing, and proud to testify

760
00:45:10,875 --> 00:45:13,344
that he was wearing
this belt buckle

761
00:45:13,477 --> 00:45:16,914
when he left her house
and went to Ann's house.

762
00:45:17,048 --> 00:45:21,645
She remembers specifically
because he bragged on it.

763
00:45:21,786 --> 00:45:27,555
And yet, when the police found his
body, he wasn't wearing a belt at all.

764
00:45:27,692 --> 00:45:30,685
We wondered about that.

765
00:45:30,828 --> 00:45:33,093
You took it off him, didn't you?

766
00:45:33,231 --> 00:45:34,961
After you killed him.

767
00:45:35,099 --> 00:45:39,036
Because, like you say, this belt
buckle means more to you than anything,

768
00:45:39,170 --> 00:45:45,235
and you weren't about to see
him with it for all eternity, were you?

769
00:45:52,817 --> 00:45:54,911
Has the jury reached a verdict?

770
00:45:55,052 --> 00:45:56,645
We have, Your Honor.

771
00:45:56,787 --> 00:45:58,983
We find the defendant
Ann Johnson...

772
00:46:02,660 --> 00:46:04,128
not guilty.

773
00:46:12,536 --> 00:46:14,129
Hey, that's me.

774
00:46:14,272 --> 00:46:16,241
I call him Justice Man.

775
00:46:16,374 --> 00:46:18,240
Got super powers, huh?

776
00:46:18,376 --> 00:46:20,641
Yeah. He can generate
a force field around him

777
00:46:20,778 --> 00:46:23,714
that only truth and
justice can penetrate.

778
00:46:23,848 --> 00:46:26,181
Oh, my gosh, I almost forgot.

779
00:46:29,687 --> 00:46:31,019
What's that?

780
00:46:31,155 --> 00:46:32,453
It's a cape.

781
00:46:32,590 --> 00:46:34,286
Why does he need a cape?

782
00:46:34,425 --> 00:46:38,863
Because Justice Man can fly.

